# THE SUBMIT GESTURE (docs/submit-plan.md §5): Return in an entry and a
# search field publishes `submitted` with the field's text; a plain
# textarea's Return is its newline and publishes nothing; a textarea that
# says `submits` publishes on Return and keeps its text. The app writes
# each submit into a label, which is the observation — an occurrence is
# asserted by what the app did with it, like every other.
expect label@sent "sent: -"
click entry@name
expect_focused entry@name
type "milk"
press return
expect label@sent "sent: milk"
# On iOS the platform's Return on a single-line field ends editing, so
# the iOS lane drops the next step (tools/lib/lanes/ios.py MODS, S3);
# it is spelled by index so the drop can name it apart from the one
# above (entry#0 is entry@name).
expect entry@name "milk"
expect_focused entry#0
click search@find
type "eggs"
press return
expect label@sent "sent: eggs"
expect search@find "eggs"
# A plain textarea: Return inserts the newline, and nothing is sent.
click textarea@plain
type "a"
press return
type "b"
expect textarea@plain "a\nb"
expect label@sent "sent: eggs"
# A submitting textarea: Return sends and inserts nothing, and the
# field keeps its focus on every platform (S3): the compose case.
click textarea@compose
type "hello"
press return
expect label@sent "sent: hello"
expect textarea@compose "hello"
expect_focused textarea@compose
# A stamped entry in a row: the copy's key rides the submit.
click entry@reply[r2]
type "yes"
press return
expect label@sent "sent: r2: yes"
